EXT. DRAINED RESERVOIR BED - DUSK

Gray fog. Cracked mud. A yellow school bus sits dead in the middle of the dry lakebed, hazards ticking.

INT. SCHOOL BUS - CONTINUOUS

Seats torn, windows fogged. MARGIT, 17, in a track jacket, crouches in the aisle with a flashlight. DEX, 18, driver's license barely dry, grips the wheel. LOLA, 12, his sister, sits in the first seat, knees to chest.

                    DEX
          Engine's dead.

                    MARGIT
          Try again.

He turns the key. Click. Click.

                    LOLA
          Dex. I don't like this.

                    DEX
          Lola, it's a bus. We're fine.

                    MARGIT
          Dex. Look.

Out the windshield, shapes in the fog. Tall. Still. A row of figures standing on the lakebed, facing the bus.

                    DEX
          Scarecrows.

                    MARGIT
          Not scarecrows.

                    LOLA
          They're closer.

Dex slaps the wheel. He locks the door and cranks the key. Click. Click. Margit kills the flashlight. The figures do not move. The fog thickens.

A tap on the glass. MR. PELL, sixty, their old bus driver, in his gray uniform, stands at the door with a lantern. His lips are blue.

                    MR. PELL
          Kids. Open up.

                    DEX
          Mr. Pell? You retired.

                    MR. PELL
          I did.

Margit looks at his boots. They are covered in dry mud, but his footprints end at the bus step. No prints behind him.

                    MARGIT
          (whispering)
          Dex. Don't.

Lola grabs her sleeve. Dex's hand hovers over the door lever.

                    MR. PELL
          It's cold, Dex. Open up.
